1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,000
Hey everyone! Welcome back to the VentureX series.

2
00:00:05,200 --> 00:00:10,000
So you've got VentureX installed locally, you've explored the CRM,
inventory, even the AI features —

3
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:13,500
and now you're ready to take it live.

4
00:00:13,700 --> 00:00:18,000
In this video, I'm going to walk you through deploying
VentureX on a shared hosting account using cPanel.

5
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:21,000
No terminal commands, no server admin degree required.

6
00:00:21,200 --> 00:00:24,000
If you can upload a file, you can do this.

7
00:00:24,200 --> 00:00:26,000
Let's get started.

8
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Before we jump in, here's what you'll need.

9
00:00:30,200 --> 00:00:34,500
First, a shared hosting account — any provider
that gives you cPanel works.

10
00:00:34,700 --> 00:00:38,000
I'm using Hostinger here, but GoDaddy, Bluehost,
SiteGround, they all look basically the same.

11
00:00:38,200 --> 00:00:43,000
Second, you need PHP 8.2 or higher enabled on your hosting.

12
00:00:43,200 --> 00:00:47,000
Most hosts have this now, but if yours is stuck on PHP 7,
you'll need to upgrade in your hosting dashboard.

13
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:51,000
Third, MySQL 5.7 or higher — again,
most hosts include this by default.

14
00:00:51,200 --> 00:00:55,000
And finally, your VentureX files ready to download.

15
00:00:55,200 --> 00:00:59,000
You can grab the latest release from the GitHub repository
or the official download link.

16
00:00:59,200 --> 00:01:01,000
Got all four? Great. Let's open cPanel.

17
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Alright, here's your cPanel dashboard.

18
00:01:06,200 --> 00:01:10,000
Look for the File Manager icon — it's usually
under the Files section.

19
00:01:10,200 --> 00:01:12,000
Click that.

20
00:01:12,200 --> 00:01:16,000
Now you'll see your hosting file structure.

21
00:01:16,200 --> 00:01:20,000
Navigate to public_html — that's your website's root folder.

22
00:01:20,200 --> 00:01:24,000
If there's an index.php or default.html in there from your host,
you can delete it.

23
00:01:24,200 --> 00:01:27,000
Now click Upload at the top.

24
00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:30,000
You'll see a file upload page.

25
00:01:30,200 --> 00:01:34,000
Drag your VentureX zip file right into the upload area,
or click Select File.

26
00:01:34,200 --> 00:01:38,000
Wait for it to finish — the bar should turn green at 100%.

27
00:01:38,200 --> 00:01:43,000
Head back to File Manager, find the zip file,
right-click it, and choose Extract.

28
00:01:43,200 --> 00:01:47,000
Extract it right into public_html.

29
00:01:47,200 --> 00:01:51,000
If your zip has a folder inside like 'venturex-main',
you'll want to move everything from that folder up to public_html directly.

30
00:01:51,200 --> 00:01:55,000
I'll show you — click into the folder, Select All,
then Move, and type public_html as the destination.

31
00:01:55,200 --> 00:01:58,000
Now your files are in the right place.

32
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:02,000
File uploaded. Next, we need a database.

33
00:02:02,200 --> 00:02:06,000
Back in cPanel, find MySQL Database Wizard —
it walks you through the whole process.

34
00:02:06,200 --> 00:02:10,000
Step one: name your database. I'll call mine venturex_prod.

35
00:02:10,200 --> 00:02:12,000
Click Next Step.

36
00:02:12,200 --> 00:02:16,000
Step two: create a database user. I'll use vxuser.

37
00:02:16,200 --> 00:02:20,000
Generate a strong password — click the Password Generator button,
copy that password somewhere safe, you'll need it.

38
00:02:20,200 --> 00:02:24,000
Check the box to confirm you saved it, then Create User.

39
00:02:24,200 --> 00:02:28,000
Step three: add the user to the database.
Check ALL PRIVILEGES —

40
00:02:28,200 --> 00:02:31,000
that gives your app full access to its own database.

41
00:02:31,200 --> 00:02:33,000
Click Next Step.

42
00:02:33,200 --> 00:02:36,000
Done! Write down your database name, username, and password.

43
00:02:36,200 --> 00:02:38,000
You'll need all three in a minute.

44
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:43,000
Now here's where some people get stuck.

45
00:02:43,200 --> 00:02:47,000
VentureX uses Composer to install its PHP dependencies.

46
00:02:47,200 --> 00:02:51,000
If your host has cPanel Terminal — that's the black icon
that looks like a command line — you can use that.

47
00:02:51,200 --> 00:02:53,000
Open it.

48
00:02:53,200 --> 00:02:57,000
You'll need to be in your project directory,
so type: cd public_html.

49
00:02:57,200 --> 00:03:00,000
Then run: composer install.

50
00:03:00,200 --> 00:03:04,000
If your host doesn't have cPanel Terminal,
or if Composer isn't available, here's the workaround.

51
00:03:04,200 --> 00:03:08,000
Some hosts let you run Composer from the PHP version selector area,
or you can check if there's a 'Composer' app in cPanel under Software.

52
00:03:08,200 --> 00:03:13,000
If none of that works, don't panic.

53
00:03:13,200 --> 00:03:18,000
You can run composer install on your local machine,
upload the entire vendor folder via File Manager,

54
00:03:18,200 --> 00:03:20,000
and it works exactly the same way.

55
00:03:20,200 --> 00:03:25,000
The vendor folder contains all the pre-installed dependencies —
just zip it up locally after running composer install,

56
00:03:25,200 --> 00:03:28,000
upload the zip, extract it in public_html, and you're good.

57
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:32,000
Time to configure your environment file.

58
00:03:32,200 --> 00:03:36,000
In File Manager, find the .env file in your project root.
Right-click, Edit.

59
00:03:36,200 --> 00:03:40,000
If you don't see a .env file,
look for .env.example — rename it to .env.

60
00:03:40,200 --> 00:03:43,000
Now let's fill in the values.

61
00:03:43,200 --> 00:03:47,000
First, APP_URL — set this to your domain,
like https://yourdomain.com.

62
00:03:47,200 --> 00:03:50,000
APP_ENV should be 'production'.

63
00:03:50,200 --> 00:03:52,000
APP_KEY — you can leave this blank for now, we'll generate it.

64
00:03:52,200 --> 00:03:55,000
DB_CONNECTION is mysql.

65
00:03:55,200 --> 00:03:58,000
DB_HOST is usually localhost on shared hosting.

66
00:03:58,200 --> 00:04:00,000
DB_PORT is 3306.

67
00:04:00,200 --> 00:04:04,000
DB_DATABASE — that's the database name you just created.

68
00:04:04,200 --> 00:04:07,000
DB_USERNAME — that's the user you created.

69
00:04:07,200 --> 00:04:11,000
DB_PASSWORD — paste in that strong password you saved.

70
00:04:11,200 --> 00:04:15,000
Scroll down to MAIL settings if you want email notifications to work —
set MAIL_HOST, MAIL_PORT, MAIL_USERNAME, and MAIL_PASSWORD

71
00:04:15,200 --> 00:04:18,000
with your email provider's SMTP details.

72
00:04:18,200 --> 00:04:20,000
Save the file.

73
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:23,000
Almost there. We need to generate the application encryption key.

74
00:04:23,200 --> 00:04:27,000
Back in cPanel Terminal, in your public_html directory,
run: php artisan key:generate.

75
00:04:27,200 --> 00:04:30,000
You should see a green success message.

76
00:04:30,200 --> 00:04:34,000
If you can't access Terminal, here's the trick —
you can generate a key locally using the same command,

77
00:04:34,200 --> 00:04:38,000
then copy the 32-character string it gives you
and paste it into the APP_KEY field in your .env file.

78
00:04:38,200 --> 00:04:42,000
Just make sure to add base64: before the key in the .env file.

79
00:04:42,200 --> 00:04:44,000
Save and close.

80
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:48,000
VentureX needs a couple of scheduled tasks to work properly —

81
00:04:48,200 --> 00:04:51,000
things like sending email reminders, cleaning up old data,

82
00:04:51,200 --> 00:04:54,000
and running AI queue jobs.

83
00:04:54,200 --> 00:04:57,000
In cPanel, find Cron Jobs.

84
00:04:57,200 --> 00:05:01,000
You'll see a section called Add New Cron Job.

85
00:05:01,200 --> 00:05:05,000
Set the timing to Every Minute —
that's the asterisk notation: * * * * *.

86
00:05:05,200 --> 00:05:09,000
Then in the command field, type:
cd /home/yourusername/public_html && php artisan schedule:run >> /dev/null 2>&1.

87
00:05:09,200 --> 00:05:13,000
Replace yourusername with your actual cPanel username.

88
00:05:13,200 --> 00:05:15,000
Click Add New Cron Job.

89
00:05:15,200 --> 00:05:18,000
That's it — your scheduler is running.

90
00:05:18,200 --> 00:05:22,000
Some hosts have a minimum of 1 minute,
some only allow 5 minutes.

91
00:05:22,200 --> 00:05:25,000
If 1 minute isn't available, 5 minutes works fine
for most use cases.

92
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:29,000
Let's secure your site with HTTPS.

93
00:05:29,200 --> 00:05:33,000
Most cPanel hosts include free SSL through Let's Encrypt.

94
00:05:33,200 --> 00:05:37,000
Find the SSL/TLS section, or look for
'Let's Encrypt' or 'SSL Certificates' in your dashboard.

95
00:05:37,200 --> 00:05:41,000
Select your domain, choose the Let's Encrypt option,
and click Issue or Install.

96
00:05:41,200 --> 00:05:43,000
It takes about 30 seconds.

97
00:05:43,200 --> 00:05:47,000
Once it's installed, go back to your .env file
and make sure APP_URL starts with https://, not http://.

98
00:05:47,200 --> 00:05:51,000
If you don't see the SSL option in cPanel,
contact your host — some require you to enable it from the hosting dashboard first.

99
00:05:51,200 --> 00:05:55,000
After SSL is active, try visiting your site.
You should see the padlock icon in the browser.

100
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:58,000
Moment of truth — open your browser
and go to your domain.

101
00:05:58,200 --> 00:06:02,000
You should see the VentureX login page!

102
00:06:02,200 --> 00:06:06,000
Log in with the default credentials
or the ones you set during local installation.

103
00:06:06,200 --> 00:06:10,000
If you see a blank page,
check that all files uploaded correctly and that your .env database details are right.

104
00:06:10,200 --> 00:06:15,000
If you get a 500 error, it's usually a permissions issue —
in File Manager, set storage and bootstrap/cache folders to 755 permissions.

105
00:06:15,200 --> 00:06:19,000
If you see 'Whoops, something went wrong,'
double-check your APP_KEY — it might be missing the base64: prefix.

106
00:06:19,200 --> 00:06:22,000
And that's it! You've just deployed VentureX on shared hosting.

107
00:06:22,200 --> 00:06:26,000
In the next video, we'll cover VPS and cloud deployment
for those who need more power.

108
00:06:26,200 --> 00:06:28,000
See you there!
